/**
 * @addtogroup Foundation_BuildGuide_AVR
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildAVRTarget Initial Supported Target
 *
 * The initial AVR export profile is:
 *
 * @code
 * atmega328p_avrgcc_avr5
 * @endcode
 *
 * It selects the AVR-GCC C/C++ toolchain, the ATmega328P MCU, and the AVR5
 * architecture. Debug and Release share one build tree; the exported package is
 * Release-only:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/export.sh atmega328p_avrgcc_avr5
 * @endcode
 *
 * The preset is an exact binary identity, not a generic “AVR” archive. The
 * toolchain passes `-mmcu=atmega328p` to C/C++ compilation and executable link
 * operations and records `avr5` in package metadata.
 *
 * @code
 * dist/atmega328p_avrgcc_avr5/
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildAVRToolchain Toolchain Configuration
 *
 * `cmake/toolchains/avr-gcc.cmake` finds `avr-gcc`, `avr-g++`, `avr-ar`, and
 * `avr-ranlib`. Set `FOUNDATION_AVR_TOOLCHAIN_ROOT` when the toolchain's `bin`
 * directory is not on `PATH`.
 *
 * A new AVR preset must use a device profile that fixes both
 * `FOUNDATION_AVR_MCU` and `FOUNDATION_AVR_ARCHITECTURE`. Do not reuse the
 * ATmega328P archive for another MCU merely because both are AVR devices.
 *
 * @warning AVR C++ standard-library support varies by toolchain distribution.
 * Foundation avoids requiring the C++ standard library, but every new profile
 * must still be compiled and consumer-tested with its intended AVR-GCC version.
 * The toolchain disables exceptions and RTTI and enables `-fcheck-new` so a
 * target allocator can report exhaustion with a null pointer.
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildAVRMemory Dynamic Memory
 *
 * AVR builds may use heap-backed APIs; disabling exceptions does not disable
 * the heap. `Matrix::Dynamic::Allocate()` reports failure with false rather
 * than throwing. Arduino AVR Core provides the required non-throwing allocation
 * functions. A bare-metal runtime must supply its own compatible `new[]` and
 * `delete[]` implementation. Applications that require deterministic memory
 * can attach a caller-owned buffer instead:
 *
 * @code{cpp}
 * float storage[9] = {};
 * Foundation::Math::Matrix::Dynamic<float> matrix;
 * const bool attached = matrix.Attach(3, 3, storage);
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildAVRCommands Complete Local Sequence
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * avr-gcc --version
 * avr-g++ --version
 * ./scripts/configure.sh atmega328p_avrgcc_avr5 --fresh
 * ./scripts/build.sh atmega328p_avrgcc_avr5 \
 *     --config Debug --target Foundation
 * ./scripts/build.sh atmega328p_avrgcc_avr5 \
 *     --config Release --target Foundation
 * ./scripts/export.sh atmega328p_avrgcc_avr5 --fresh
 * @endcode
 *
 * The expected package archive is `libFoundation.a`. It must report `elf32-avr`
 * and architecture `avr:5`; no Debug archive is distributed:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * avr-objdump -f dist/atmega328p_avrgcc_avr5/lib/libFoundation.a
 * test ! -e dist/atmega328p_avrgcc_avr5/lib/libFoundation_d.a
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildAVRConsumer Consumer Validation
 *
 * Configure a separate AVR CMake project with the same ATmega328P toolchain,
 * point `CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH` at the export, call
 * `find_package(Foundation CONFIG REQUIRED)`, and link
 * `Foundation::Foundation`. The consumer must call a non-inline Foundation
 * function so the linker actually reads the archive. Inspect the resulting ELF
 * with `avr-size`, `avr-nm`, and `avr-objdump` before running it on hardware.
 *
 * Repository CTest is not an AVR execution environment. Behavioral tests require
 * an AVR emulator or board integration. See @ref Foundation_Workflows_Validation.
 */
